import pytest

@pytest.fixture(scope="session")
def pg_container():
    c = start_postgres()            # 8 s, once per run
    yield c
    c.stop()

@pytest.fixture(scope="session", params=["postgres", "sqlite"])
def engine(request, pg_container):
    if request.param == "sqlite":
        yield make_engine("sqlite:///:memory:")
    else:
        yield make_engine(pg_container.dsn)   # matrix: every dependent test runs twice

@pytest.fixture
def db(engine):
    conn = engine.connect()
    tx = conn.begin()
    yield conn                      # tests see ONE isolated transaction
    tx.rollback()                   # teardown runs even on failure
    conn.close()

@pytest.fixture
def make_user(db):
    created = []
    def _make(plan="free", **kw):
        u = insert_user(db, plan=plan, **kw)
        created.append(u)
        return u
    yield _make
    # rollback already isolates; explicit reversed-cleanup kept for non-tx resources

The budget math drives the scopes: container and engine are session-scoped (8 s once, not 53 min), and isolation comes from the function-scoped db fixture whose transaction rolls back in teardown — every test sees a pristine database for the cost of begin/rollback, single-digit milliseconds. The invariant making this safe is that nothing mutable and session-scoped is ever handed to a test: engine is shared but treated as immutable; all writes happen inside the per-test transaction. The backend matrix lives in params= on the engine fixture, so all 400 tests run against both backends with no duplicated code — reported as test_x[postgres] / test_x[sqlite]. Plan variation goes through the make_user factory, not through mutating a shared tenant — the order-dependence class from the lesson. The only autouse fixture is environment hygiene (monkeypatch already auto-undoes; an autouse fixture that snapshots os.environ guards against tests using os.environ directly), justified because cross-cutting isolation is the one concern where invisibility beats explicitness.

